1. kube-api server (Front door & single source of truth)
Every command (kubectl, scheduler, controllers) talks only to this.
Reads/writes cluster state to etcd.
If API server is down → cluster is blind.
👉 Rule of thumb: Nothing bypasses the API server.

2. etcd (Cluster memory)
Key-value database.
Stores desired state: Pods, Deployments, ConfigMaps, Secrets.
If etcd is lost → cluster amnesia 😬.
👉 This is why etcd backups are non-negotiable in prod.

3. scheduler (Placement strategist)
Watches for Pending Pods.
Decides which worker node a Pod should run on.
Factors: CPU, memory, taints/tolerations, affinity.
👉 Scheduler does not run pods—it only assigns them.

4. controller manager (Autopilot)
Runs controllers like:
Node controller
ReplicaSet controller
Deployment controller
Constantly compares:
desired state vs actual state
If mismatch → corrective action.
👉 Example: Pod dies → controller creates a new one. Old-school ops, automated.

